{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Field evidence: a store write-lock holder exceeded 60s under normal parallel agent work\n\nObserved 2026-08-05 ~01:45 UTC during a session running several agents in parallel against\nthis store (each claiming, commenting, and closing tickets).\n\nA close of d772-6007-5d04-4101 failed with:\n\n    rebar._commands._seam.CommandError: flock: could not acquire lock after 60s\n    (raised from llm/completion_sidecar.py:60 emit -> _commands/_seam.py:419 append_event)\n\nThis is direct evidence for this ticket's premise: some holder retained the store write lock\nfor longer than the full 60s acquisition window, starving a concurrent writer outright rather\nthan merely slowing it. The starved operation was a completion-sidecar append, i.e. gate\nmachinery, not bulk work.\n\nCorroborating signal from the same session: an earlier `rebar review-plan` on\n43ea-3d5a-078f-4fe3 computed a PASS verdict but ABORTED signing with reason\n'store-read-failure' (plan_review_sign_aborted, attempt 1), and the attestation had to be\nrecovered separately with `rebar sign-review`. Same root condition -- contention against the\nstore -- surfacing in a second subsystem.\n\nNeither failure needed an unusual workload to reproduce: it was ordinary parallel agent\nactivity, which is the workflow AGENTS.md actively recommends."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785894720427099001,"uuid":"7b89ee94-65f4-4431-bd68-d479b602a71c"}